## Horizon

The `worker` service runs [Laravel Horizon](https://laravel.com/docs/horizon), which supervises the Redis queue workers; its settings (supervisors, processes, timeouts, retries) live in `config/horizon.php`.

Dashboard: [`http://localhost:{{ app_port }}/horizon`](http://localhost:{{ app_port }}/horizon)

Outside the `local` environment (staging, production) the dashboard is only reachable by users allowed by the `viewHorizon` gate in `app/Providers/HorizonServiceProvider.php`. It ships with an empty list of allowed emails, so add yours there before deploying.

If a job can run longer than 60 s, raise `timeout` for its supervisor in `config/horizon.php` (keeping `retry_after` in `config/queue.php` above it) **and** raise `WORKER_STOP_GRACE_PERIOD` above it: otherwise `docker compose stop worker` kills the job once the grace period runs out.
{% endif %}

## Deploying staging and production

`docker-compose.stage.yml` and `docker-compose.prod.yml` are two Compose projects (`{{ app_slug }}-stage` and `{{ app_slug }}-prod`) with their own containers and volumes, so both can run from the same checkout without replacing each other. Each file's header explains where its `${VAR}` settings come from. Two ways to deploy:

```bash
# build the image on the host
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build

# or use an image pushed by CI
export APP_IMAGE=<registry>/<image> IMAGE_TAG=<tag>
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --no-build
```

The image is `${APP_IMAGE:-{{ app_slug }}}:${IMAGE_TAG:-latest}`, i.e. `{{ app_slug }}:latest` when neither variable is set. Keep both set (exported, or in the env file) for every command that starts containers: with only `pull` given them, `up` would look for `{{ app_slug }}:latest`.
{%- if queue != 'sync' %} `app` is the only service that builds or pulls the image; `worker` reuses it (`pull_policy: never`), so only one build ever runs.{% endif %}

{% if proxy_network %}Neither stack publishes a host port, so `APP_PORT` has no effect: `app` is reached through the reverse proxy on the `{{ proxy_network }}` network (see [Reverse proxy and TLS](#reverse-proxy-and-tls)).{% else %}Both stacks listen on the host port `APP_PORT`, which defaults to `8000` for production and `8001` for staging so they can start together; set `APP_PORT` to move either.{% endif %} Staging and production are separate environments, so give each its own secrets with `--env-file` rather than one shared `.env`.
