## CI pipeline

`.github/workflows/docker-publish.yml` builds the production `Dockerfile` and publishes the image to the GitHub Container Registry as `ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>` (lowercase). A push publishes it; a pull request only builds it, to check that the image still builds.

| Trigger | Tags pushed |
|---|---|
| Push to `main`, `master` or `develop` | `sha-<short sha>`, the branch name (slashes become `-`), and `latest` on the repository's default branch |
| Push of a `v*.*.*` tag | `sha-<short sha>`, `stable`, and the version: `v1.2.3` is also tagged `1.2.3`, `1.2` and `1` |
| Pull request into one of those branches | none (build only) |

Deploy a pushed tag with the stack of your choice (`docker-compose.stage.yml` works the same way):

```bash
export APP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo> IMAGE_TAG=<tag>
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --no-build
```

If the package is private, run `docker login ghcr.io` on the deploy host first, with a token that has `read:packages`.
{% elif ci_provider == 'gitlab' %}
## CI pipeline

`.gitlab-ci.yml` builds the production `Dockerfile` with Docker-in-Docker (`docker:27-cli` and a `docker:27-dind` service, so the runner must allow privileged containers) and pushes the image to the project's Container Registry, `$CI_REGISTRY_IMAGE` (`<registry>/<group>/<project>`). Every branch and tag pipeline publishes it.

| Pipeline | Tags pushed |
|---|---|
| Default branch | `<short sha>` and `latest` |
| Any other branch | `<short sha>` and the branch slug (`CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG`) |
| Git tag | `<short sha>`, the tag name (`v1.2.3`) and `stable` |

Deploy a pushed tag with the stack of your choice (`docker-compose.stage.yml` works the same way):

```bash
export APP_IMAGE=<registry>/<group>/<project> IMAGE_TAG=<tag>
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml pull
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --no-build
```

If the project is private, run `docker login <registry>` on the deploy host first, with a deploy token that has `read_registry`.
{% endif %}
## Reverse proxy and TLS
{% if proxy_network %}
The stacks serve plain HTTP and do not terminate TLS: a TLS-terminating reverse proxy (Traefik, Caddy, nginx-proxy, …) sits in front of them. This project was generated with `proxy_network={{ proxy_network }}`, so in both stacks `app` publishes no host port and joins the external Docker network `{{ proxy_network }}` instead, under a per-environment alias. Every other service stays on the stack's own network, out of the proxy's reach.

1. Create the network once on the host, unless the proxy's own stack already did, and attach the proxy to it:

   ```bash
   docker network create {{ proxy_network }}
   ```

   It is declared `external`, so Compose neither creates nor removes it: `docker compose up` fails if it does not exist, and `down` leaves it alone.
2. Start the stacks as described above.
3. Route each domain to the alias of its environment, on port 80. Do not use `app`: Compose also registers the service name on every network the service joins, so `app` resolves to the `app` of both stacks (and of any other project on the network), and a proxy using it would balance between environments.

   | Environment | Alias | Upstream for the proxy |
   |---|---|---|
   | Staging | `{{ app_slug }}-stage` | `http://{{ app_slug }}-stage:80` |
   | Production | `{{ app_slug }}-prod` | `http://{{ app_slug }}-prod:80` |

Staging and production can join the network at the same time, because their aliases differ.

Have the proxy send `X-Forwarded-Proto`, `X-Forwarded-Host` and `X-Forwarded-For` (most do by default). The app trusts them (`trustProxies` in `bootstrap/app.php`), so it builds `https://` URLs and sees the real client IP. It trusts them from any sender, so only attach containers you trust to this network. Set `APP_URL` to the public `https://` URL of each environment.

To check the wiring from another container on the network (the `/up` health route answers `200`):

```bash
docker run --rm --network {{ proxy_network }} curlimages/curl -sSf -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://{{ app_slug }}-prod/up
```
{%- else %}
The stacks serve plain HTTP and do not terminate TLS. For anything beyond a trial, put a TLS-terminating reverse proxy (Traefik, Caddy, nginx, …) in front of the published port (`APP_PORT`) and point your domain at the proxy. Have it send `X-Forwarded-Proto`, `X-Forwarded-Host` and `X-Forwarded-For`: the app trusts them (`trustProxies` in `bootstrap/app.php`), so it builds `https://` URLs and sees the real client IP. It trusts them from any sender, so do not expose the port to untrusted clients directly. Set `APP_URL` to the public `https://` URL of each environment.

If the proxy runs in Docker on the same host, you can skip the published port: regenerate the project with `proxy_network=<name>` (an existing Docker network the proxy is attached to) and `app` joins that network under the alias `{{ app_slug }}-stage` / `{{ app_slug }}-prod` instead.
{%- endif %}

## Storage and persistent data

Staging and production keep their state in named volumes, so `docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml down` followed by `up -d` (without `-v`, which deletes the volumes) loses nothing. Compose prefixes each volume with the project name (`{{ app_slug }}-prod_storage_data`, `{{ app_slug }}-stage_storage_data`, …), so staging and production never share one:

| Volume | Mounted at | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| `storage_data` | `/var/www/html/storage/app` | Uploaded files (the `local` and `public` disks){% if queue != 'sync' %}. Shared by `app` and `worker`: files written by web requests are read by queued jobs{% endif %} |
{% if db == 'sqlite' -%}
| `sqlite_data` | `/var/www/html/database/data` | The SQLite database (`DB_DATABASE=/var/www/html/database/data/database.sqlite`){% if queue != 'sync' %}. Shared by `app` and `worker`; with one file per container the worker would see an empty database{% endif %} |
{% elif db == 'mysql' -%}
| `db_data` | `/var/lib/mysql` | The MySQL data |
{% else -%}
| `db_data` | `/var/lib/postgresql/data` | The PostgreSQL data |
{% endif -%}
{% if queue == 'redis' -%}
| `redis_data` | `/data` | Redis persistence (queued jobs) |
{% endif %}
On every boot `docker/prod.entrypoint.sh` re-creates the `storage/` directories, gives `www-data` (the user php-fpm{% if queue != 'sync' %} and the worker{% endif %} run as) ownership of whatever is not already its own, and creates the `public/storage` link, so files on the `public` disk are served at `/storage/<name>`. Run commands that write to `storage/` as that user (`docker compose exec -u www-data app php artisan …`); anything left root-owned is fixed on the next boot.

## Resource limits

Every service in the staging and production stacks has CPU and memory **limits** and **reservations** (`deploy.resources`), so a runaway process or a memory leak cannot starve the database and everything else on the host. Each value can be overridden from the shell or the env file, like the other settings:

| Service | Prefix | CPU limit | Memory limit | CPU reservation | Memory reservation |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `app` | `APP_` | `1.0` | `512M` | `0.25` | `256M` |
{% if queue != 'sync' -%}
| `worker` | `WORKER_` | `1.0` | `512M` | `0.25` | `256M` |
{% endif -%}
{% if db == 'mysql' -%}
| `db` (MySQL) | `DB_` | `1.0` | `1G` | `0.25` | `512M` |
{% elif db == 'postgres' -%}
| `db` (PostgreSQL) | `DB_` | `1.0` | `512M` | `0.25` | `256M` |
{% endif -%}
{% if queue == 'redis' -%}
| `redis` | `REDIS_` | `0.5` | `256M` | `0.1` | `128M` |
{% endif %}
The variables are `<PREFIX>CPU_LIMIT`, `<PREFIX>MEM_LIMIT`, `<PREFIX>CPU_RESERVATION` and `<PREFIX>MEM_RESERVATION`, and each one changes only its own service:

```bash
# give `app` 1 GB of memory; every other service keeps its defaults
APP_MEM_LIMIT=1G docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d
```
{% if db == 'mysql' %}
MySQL 8 gets higher defaults than the other services because it can exceed 512M while starting.
{% endif %}
- A limit is a hard cap: a container over its memory limit is killed (and restarted, `restart: unless-stopped`), one over its CPU limit is throttled. If a service keeps restarting under load, check `docker inspect <container>` for `"OOMKilled": true` and raise its memory limit. `docker stats --no-stream` shows the limit next to the current usage.
- The memory reservation is a soft guarantee: Docker only enforces it when the host runs short of memory. The CPU reservation is accepted by Compose, but Docker Engine does not apply it outside Swarm. `docker inspect` shows the CPU and memory limits and the memory reservation (`HostConfig` → `NanoCpus`, `Memory`, `MemoryReservation`).
- Docker refuses to create a container whose memory reservation is above its limit (`Minimum memory limit can not be less than memory reservation limit`), so when you lower a limit below the default reservation, lower the reservation too: `APP_MEM_LIMIT=128M APP_MEM_RESERVATION=64M`.

### Hosts that cannot apply limits

Some hosts cannot apply cgroup limits, for example some Docker-in-Docker setups, where the containers fail to start with `cannot enter cgroupv2 ... in threaded mode`. There the limits can be dropped with an override file, here `docker-compose.no-limits.yml` (`!reset` needs Docker Compose 2.24 or later):

```yaml
services:
  app: {deploy: !reset {}}
{%- if queue != 'sync' %}
  worker: {deploy: !reset {}}
{%- endif %}
{%- if db != 'sqlite' %}
  db: {deploy: !reset {}}
{%- endif %}
{%- if queue == 'redis' %}
  redis: {deploy: !reset {}}
{%- endif %}
```

Pass it after the stack file in every command that starts containers:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml -f docker-compose.no-limits.yml up -d
```

